% PDD prompt for audio.py — Lane 2, mock + real streaming version

Write audio.py, a Python module exposing exactly one function:
    def get_utterance() -> dict:

## Mock path (USE_MOCK = True) — this behavior must be preserved exactly

- A module-level constant USE_MOCK = True at the top of the file.
- When USE_MOCK is True, get_utterance() replays a hardcoded list of
  utterances, one per call, and makes no network call.
- The hardcoded list, in this exact order:
  1. {"text": "Are you guys SOC 2 certified?", "speaker": "prospect", "ts": 252.4}
  2. A follow-up utterance asking whether it's Type I or Type II, speaker
     "prospect", with a later ts.
  3. A small-talk utterance about the weekend, speaker "prospect", with a
     later ts.
  4. A pricing question, speaker "prospect", with a later ts.
  ts values must strictly increase across the list.
- Each call to get_utterance() returns the next utterance in the list,
  advancing an internal index. Each utterance is returned exactly once.
- Once the list is exhausted, every subsequent call returns
  EMPTY = {"text": "", "speaker": "unknown", "ts": 0.0}.
- Include a __main__ block that calls get_utterance() six times and prints
  each result, so exhaustion of the 4-item list is visible.

## Real path (USE_MOCK = False) — ElevenLabs Scribe v2 Realtime

- On first use, start a background thread that opens a WebSocket connection
  to wss://api.elevenlabs.io/v1/speech-to-text/stream, authenticating with
  header xi-api-key set to ELEVENLABS_API_KEY from the environment
  (use os.environ / python-dotenv).
- The background thread captures microphone audio using sounddevice and
  sends it as input_audio_chunk messages over the WebSocket. Use VAD-based
  automatic commit (not manual commit).
- The background thread listens for incoming messages. Discard every
  partial transcript. When a committed transcript arrives, push
  {"text": <transcript>, "speaker": <diarized label if confidently
  available, otherwise "unknown">, "ts": <seconds since this session
  started>} onto a thread-safe queue.Queue.
- get_utterance() itself never touches the network directly. In the real
  path it only pops from the queue with a non-blocking get
  (queue.get_nowait() wrapped in try/except queue.Empty). If the queue is
  empty, return EMPTY immediately — never block.
- If the WebSocket connection drops, the background thread must
  automatically attempt to reconnect with a short backoff, while
  get_utterance() keeps returning EMPTY (never raises) during the outage.
- Add an optional benchmark flag via environment variable AUDIO_BENCHMARK.
  When set to "1", measure and print, in milliseconds, the latency from
  when an audio chunk is sent to when its corresponding committed
  transcript is returned by get_utterance().
- Do not implement any custom sentence-boundary or utterance-splitting
  logic — treat each committed transcript from the API as one complete
  utterance.

## Applies to both paths

- get_utterance() must return immediately and never block the calling
  thread in either path.
- Any internal error (mic unavailable, auth failure, malformed message,
  connection error, or anything else) must be caught and EMPTY returned —
  get_utterance() must never raise under any circumstance.
- The module must print one line per call in the form:
  [audio] out=<text>

## Must not

- Must not raise under any circumstance, in either path.
- Must not block the calling thread.
- Must not return the same utterance twice.
- Must not return partial transcripts in the real path.
- Must not import websockets/requests/sounddevice usage inside the mock
  path — those imports and their use are confined to the real path only.

Add sounddevice and websocket-client (or websockets) imports as needed for
the real path. Keep the file plain — minimal classes are fine only if
needed for the WebSocket thread/queue management, no unnecessary
abstraction.